# oe-topics.env — the ONE definition of what gets archived, and of which job archives it.
#
# Sourced by oe-archive-kafka.sh (topic defaults), oe-archive-daily.sh (nightly wrapper),
# oe-archive-dev.sh (2-hourly dev capture) and oe-archive-verify.sh (completeness check).
# Previously each carried its own copy of the same names: equal today, free to drift tomorrow —
# and two environments archiving different topic sets do not produce a comparison, they produce two
# unrelated datasets that merely look comparable. Codex flagged exactly that on 2026-08-08:
# "current equality, not structural prevention".
#
# Change a set HERE and every caller changes with it. Adding a topic is safe (the archiver skips
# topics that do not exist); removing one silently ends its capture, so remove deliberately.
# context-tape.direction.ledger joins this set (A5, 2026-09-08): it is the ONLY dev path that
# archives the calibration corpus. Both dev archive invocations pass TOPICS="$DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE",
# so a ledger absent from this list is a dev corpus that is never captured at all — the dev target
# declaration and the gateway relay would have had nothing behind them (r12 #4).
DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE="dealer-ledger-signal-fired dealer-ledger-outcome-scored dealer-ledger-state dealer-ledger-profile dealer-ledger-calibration-state context-tape.direction.ledger"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JOB SPLIT — why these two sets are DISJOINT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-12: the daily-heavy archive produced nothing for env=prod on 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11
# while the 10-minute spot archive stayed current. Both called oe-archive-kafka.sh with ENV=prod and
# the same ARCHIVE_DIR, so both computed the SAME lock path (/tmp/oe-archive-kafka.prod.<hash>.lock).
# The 10-minute job held it at 17:10; the daily job logged "exiting clean" and returned rc=0.
# Nothing alerted, because exit 0 was being read as proof of archival.
#
# The lock itself was right: two writers into one <topic>.offsets checkpoint file can each advance
# past a range the other never wrote, and that is a GAP. The defect was that the lock's SCOPE was
# the whole environment when its real subject is a TOPIC.
#
# Fixed in two independent layers, either of which alone would have prevented this:
#   1. POLICY (here): the two jobs own disjoint topic sets, so they have nothing to serialise on.
#   2. MECHANISM (oe-archive-kafka.sh): locks are per job AND per topic, so an ad-hoc run with an
#      overlapping list still cannot corrupt a manifest — and cannot starve a scheduled job either.
# Keep these sets disjoint. If they must overlap, layer 2 keeps it correct, but the overlapping
# topic gets skipped by whichever job arrives second and only layer 1 guarantees promptness.

# SPOT — owned by the */10 cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=spot).
# These must be archived far more often than once a day: underlying.spx.price is
# cleanup.policy=compact,delete with min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.01 and segment.ms=1h, so a closed
# segment is compacted almost at once — on 2026-08-03 only 3,306 of 804,629 offsets were still
# readable. Only the ACTIVE segment is safe from the cleaner.
OE_SPOT_TOPICS="underlying.spx.price underlying.spx.index.price underlying.es.price spx.basis.state"

# RECONCILED FROM THE HOST 2026-09-09. This list was widened on .252 on 2026-08-23 and the change was
# never brought back into git, so the repo still held the pre-widening set: deploying it would have cut
# the prod nightly archive from 184 topics to about 17, silently. The drift check refused, which is the
# only reason anyone found out. The host's list is authoritative here and is taken verbatim, plus the
# A5 ledger.
# HEAVY — owned by the 17:10 ET daily wrapper (ARCHIVE_JOB=daily). Multi-million-record topics
# that are written once per session and are pointless to poll every 10 minutes.
#
# 2026-08-12 additions: delta-flow-by-strike and option-price-behavior-by-strike. These are the two
# primary inputs to near-strike bias (NEAR-STRIKE-BIAS-GATE1.md) and they were archived NOWHERE, in
# any environment, against a 1-day Kafka retention — every session of both was being destroyed
# nightly. Both are plain JSON on 32 partitions and carry their own tradingDate.
# 2026-08-23: set widened from 11 topics to 183 — USER: "archive all the topic except
# changelog topic and offset topic". Excluded on purpose (139 topics): Kafka internals
# (__consumer_offsets, __transaction_state, _schemas) and Kafka Streams internals —
# *-changelog, *-rekey, *-repartition. Streams internals are derivable from their source
# topics and are NOT evidence; a changelog also has to stay cleanup.policy=compact, because
# a state store is restored by replaying it whole and a time-truncated changelog restores a
# SILENTLY incomplete store. Adding a topic here is safe (the archiver skips absent ones).
OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod="close.direction.signal close.direction.state corridor-gauge-event-log corridor-gauge-state dealer-ledger-calibration-state dealer-ledger-greeks dealer-ledger-outcome-scored dealer-ledger-profile dealer-ledger-quarantine dealer-ledger-signal-fired dealer-ledger-state delta-flow-10s delta-flow-15m delta-flow-1m delta-flow-30s delta-flow-5m delta-flow-by-strike delta-flow-by-trade delta-flow-corrections delta-flow-dashboard delta-flow-diagnostics delta-flow-session delta-flow-trades display.volume.current display.volume.sandwich.alerts display.volume.sandwich.current es.drop.final-summary es.drop.nowcast es.drop.outcome es.futures.aggressor-flow es.futures.cvd es.futures.cvd.bars es.futures.cvd.levels es.open-direction.forecast es.open-direction.outcome es.open-direction.status es.options.databento.gex.spxbridge es.options.indicators.bars es.options.indicators.snapshot.current es.options.marketdata.selection es.strike-intelligence-by-strike es.tape-zones.board es.underlying.es.price fullfunding.caption-evidence.ready fullfunding.vocabulary.dictionary-changed fullfunding.vocabulary.occurrence-observed futures.kyle-style-signed-flow-response-slope heartbeats hpsf.option.trades market.spx.market-carry-service.current market.spx.market-carry-service.history mm2-configs.es4.internal mm2-offsets.es4.internal mm2-status.es4.internal option-price-behavior-by-option option-price-behavior-by-strike option-price-behavior-config option-price-behavior-dashboard option-price-behavior-service-prod-contrib option-price-behavior-service-prod-greek-rk option-price-behavior-service-prod-strike-contrib option-price-behavior-service-prod-trade-rk option-price-behavior-session options.databento.control options.databento.directional-pressure options.databento.display options.databento.display.gamma.history options.databento.display.volume.current options.databento.enriched.raw options.databento.events.raw options.databento.gex options.databento.gex-strike options.databento.gex.flow.10s options.databento.gex.flow.15m options.databento.gex.flow.1m options.databento.gex.flow.5m options.databento.gex.flow.by-strike options.databento.gex.flow.dashboard options.databento.gex.flow.session options.databento.gex.greeks options.databento.gex.magnet options.databento.gex.oi-status options.databento.gex.strike options.databento.gex.strike-lifecycle options.databento.gex.strike.history options.databento.gex.strike.preopen options.databento.market-pressure.mission options.databento.maxpain options.databento.normalized options.databento.oi.anchor-manifest options.databento.oi.nowcast.by-strike options.databento.oi.nowcast.session-control options.databento.option.snapshot options.databento.option.trades options.databento.pace options.databento.pace.mission options.databento.pace.rank options.databento.raw options.databento.sandwich.mission options.databento.seller-activity options.databento.strike-flow options.databento.strike-flow.strike.avro options.databento.trades options.databento.volume-sandwich.alerts options.databento.volume-sandwich.current options.databento.volume.state.compacted options.delta-flow options.does.not.exist options.es-cvd-spx-levels options.es-gex-spx-aligned options.es-strike-intel-spx-aligned options.hpsf.audit options.hpsf.dlq options.hpsf.exit-signal options.hpsf.gex options.hpsf.latest-signal options.hpsf.market-flow options.hpsf.signal options.hpsf.strike-score options.hpsf.writer-dlq options.ibkr.directional-pressure options.ibkr.display options.ibkr.display.gamma.history options.ibkr.display.volume.current options.ibkr.gex.status options.ibkr.gex.strike.shadow options.ibkr.pace options.ibkr.pace.rank options.ibkr.raw options.ibkr.unusualwhales.gex.strike options.ibkr.unusualwhales.gex.strike.history options.ibkr.volume-sandwich.alerts options.ibkr.volume-sandwich.current options.indicators.bars options.indicators.bootstrap.control options.indicators.repartition options.indicators.snapshot.current options.marketdata.selection options.multileg.observations options.multileg.window.status options.opra.quotes options.opra.tcbbo options.opra.trades options.rewind.audit options.rewind.journal options.spx.delta-flow options.spx.gamma-migration.current options.spx.gamma-migration.floor-test options.spx.gamma-migration.rotation options.spx.gamma-migration.scoring options.spx.greek-move-auth.current options.spx.greek-move-auth.events options.spx.market-carry.current options.spx.mission-control.current options.spx.mission-control.current.dlq options.spx.option-truth-engine-service.by-strike options.spx.participant-flow options.spx.spot options.spx.spot-vol-regime.current options.spx.spot-vol-regime.events options.spx.spread-skew.current options.spx.spread-skew.events options.spx.strike-invasion.current options.spx.strike-invasion.events options.spx.strike-sr.current options.spx.strike-sr.history options.spx.vix-option-inteligence-service.current options.spx.wall-break-rates.dataset options.strike-liquidity-heatmap-bucket options.strike-liquidity-heatmap-dashboard rates.sofr.databento-sr3-feed-service.current rates.sofr.databento-sr3-feed-service.history signal-follower.hot-strike spx.basis.a1.evidence strike-flow strike-intelligence-by-strike strike-intelligence-dashboard strike-intelligence-turn-alert strike-liquidity-heatmap strike-liquidity-heatmap-bucket strike-liquidity-heatmap-dashboard underlying.es.trades underlying.vix.index.price underlying.vix.price context-tape.direction.ledger"

# The full prod policy = what a complete session looks like. The verifier checks THIS set; the two
# jobs above partition it. A topic that is in neither job but in this set would alert every day,
# which is the correct way to notice that a topic has no producer job.
OE_ALL_TOPICS_prod="$OE_SPOT_TOPICS $OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod"

# ES4 — owned by the 17:01 ET post-close cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=es4) on the prod host, reading the
# es4 broker directly. The cron passes NO TOPICS override (oe-archive.crontab): the archiver
# REQUIRES this variable and derives the set from it. One definition, every caller.
# 2026-08-15 addition: es.futures.cvd.bars only (ES-CVD-DESIGN.md, Gates 1-4).
#   es.futures.cvd.bars — compacted with a UNIQUE key per bar (symbol|timeframe|barStartMs), so
#     compaction is not the threat; es4's clean-reset log wipe is (the 2026-08-11 loss class).
#     This is the CVD history: one record per closed bar per timeframe, all closed by 17:00, so
#     the 17:01 capture holds exactly one whole session.
#   es.futures.cvd (the 1 Hz snapshot) is deliberately NOT here. Measured 2026-08-15 on the
#     first capture attempt: the archiver bounds a read by --max-messages == offset span and
#     relies on a 60s idle timeout to finish early — a compacted topic with a LIVE 1 Hz producer
#     satisfies neither (the span is unreachable through compaction gaps, and it never goes
#     idle), so the consumer starves until the 900s kill (rc=124) and the topic fails EVERY run,
#     forever — a daily false alarm, which is how real alarms die. Its only archival value (the
#     terminal session state) is carried by the session-timeframe bar in cvd.bars anyway.
# 2026-09-07 addition: the ES Footprint history (ES-FOOTPRINT-DESIGN.md F-R14), same loss class.
#   es.futures.footprint.bars — retention.ms=-1, UNIQUE key per closed bar (symbol|timeframe|barStartMs),
#     all closed by 17:00: one whole session per 17:01 capture (reference session: 9,122 records).
#   es.futures.footprint.outcomes — retention.ms=-1, UNIQUE key per F-E6 resolution (the identity); every
#     open item resolves at the session finalize, so the topic is complete and idle by the capture
#     (reference session: 15,339 records).
#   es.futures.footprint and es.futures.footprint.evidence (the change-driven LIVE snapshots with a
#     5 s heartbeat) are deliberately NOT here for exactly the es.futures.cvd reason above: a topic
#     with a producer that never goes idle starves the bounded read to the 900 s kill. Their archival
#     value (the last state of every timeframe) is carried by the closed bars.
#   None of the four is compacted — es4 stamps cleanup.policy=delete on every served topic — so the
#     -1 retention above, not compaction, is what makes bars/outcomes readable as a whole session.
# 2026-09-07 addition: es.futures.auction (SPX Auction Desk, es-amt-service). Plain delete with a
#   LIVE producer, so it was checked against the es.futures.cvd rule above rather than assumed:
#   the key is UNIQUE per minute (`<tradeDate>|<HH:mm>`, one record per RTH minute), nothing is
#   compacted away and the offset span is reachable, unlike the single-key 1 Hz snapshot; and
#   the producer is IDLE after 16:00 ET, so by this job's 17:01 ET run the 60s idle timeout ends the
#   read instead of the 900s kill. Both halves of the starvation hold for cvd and neither holds
#   here. What it needs archiving against is the same thing cvd.bars does: retention is seven
#   calendar days (604800000) and es4's clean-reset wipes the log outright.
# 2026-09-10 addition: es.futures.footprint.strike (ES-FOOTPRINT-STRIKE-INTERACTION.md R6/R13/R14) — the
#   strike-interaction episode log, retention.ms=-1 and retention.bytes=-1, cleanup.policy=delete (every
#   revision survives; the design puts this log in the nightly archive explicitly). A TRANSACTIONAL
#   append-only log: episode keys REPEAT across revisions and CHECKPOINT keys carry no episode at all, so
#   the archive is a log, not a last-value set; and commit/abort markers occupy offsets that never surface
#   as records, so (end - from) overstates the readable count.
#
#   CAPTURE (deploy Codex final review, 2026-09-11). This topic is in OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS
#   (oe-archive-kafka.sh), so kafka-console-consumer never reads it. StrikeArchiveReader.java takes the
#   exclusive boundary from Kafka METADATA — the read_committed end offset, i.e. the last stable offset
#   (LSO), or min(LSO, the UNTIL_TS offset) in bounded mode — writes only records below it, and reports
#   completion only when its consumer position reached it. The checkpoint and the manifest's offset_to
#   are that boundary and nothing else: a transaction still open at capture time holds the boundary
#   BELOW its first record, so those records are captured by the next run once they commit, never
#   skipped; an aborted revision is never archived; a range holding only markers or aborted records
#   completes with zero records and still advances. A reader that times out (STRIKE_READER_DEADLINE_S,
#   default 900 s), fails, or reports anything short of its boundary leaves the checkpoint where it was.
#   Rounds 2-6 recorded why no boundary may be derived from the console consumer's TEXT; this reader
#   never does, and every other topic is still captured by the console consumer exactly as before.
#
#   Completion is NOT tied to the producer going idle. The capture ends when the position reaches the
#   stable boundary taken at its start, however busy the log is afterwards; neither the 60 s idle
#   timeout nor the 17:00 finalize is part of this topic's contract. (For every console-consumer topic
#   those mechanisms are unchanged and still prove only what they prove — see the es.futures.cvd note.)
#   The floor below detects grossly missing output; it does not establish session completeness.
#
#   SOURCE PRESERVATION IS CONDITIONAL — retention -1 is NOT a repair guarantee. retention.ms=-1 and
#   retention.bytes=-1 stop RETENTION from deleting records; they do not stop a whole-broker wipe, and
#   es4 has one: scripts/es4/cleanup-es4.sh (Jenkins es4-deploy ACTION=clean-reset) deletes the entire
#   Kafka data directory, after which the same offsets name a different log. A range the archive did not
#   capture can be recaptured from the source ONLY while (1) the topic has not been wiped or re-created
#   since (its TopicId still equals the one in $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.identity) and (2) the
#   range is still inside the retained log (earliest offset <= the range start). Source preservation
#   therefore depends on the archive having COMPLETED before the wipe. The interlock that enforces it:
#   after every durable strike capture the archiver commits its boundary as the offset of consumer group
#   oe-archive-committed-boundary on the es4 broker, and cleanup-es4.sh REFUSES to wipe while that
#   offset is below the strike log end (an absent group counts as nothing archived). The only opt-out is
#   explicit — ES4_STRIKE_ARCHIVE_INTERLOCK=off (Jenkins es4-deploy ACCEPT_UNARCHIVED_STRIKE_LOSS) — and
#   the wipe then logs the loss it was told to accept.
#
#   REPAIR PROCEDURE — a range known or suspected missing from the archive, and ALWAYS before any wipe:
#     1. LOCK. Hold es4 clean-reset (the interlock refuses anyway while the marker is short), and hold
#        the archiver's own topic lock for the whole repair so no scheduled run appends meanwhile:
#        /tmp/oe-archive-kafka.es4.<cksum of ARCHIVE_DIR>.t-es.futures.footprint.strike.lock (flock).
#     2. VERIFY THE SOURCE. kafka-topics --describe must show the TopicId recorded in
#        $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.identity — a different id means the source was re-created and
#        the range is GONE: stop and record the loss. kafka-get-offsets --time earliest must be <= the
#        first offset to recapture, or the head of the range is gone the same way.
#     3. RECAPTURE conservatively, as a SUPERSET of the suspect files. For this topic the archiver resumes
#        ONLY from a checkpoint line it stamped itself ("capture=read_committed_stable_boundary", written
#        after a verified committed-read capture); the LAST line for a partition that is not stamped is
#        LEGACY, and the next run recaptures that partition from the log START (deploy re-review round 2,
#        finding 2). So the repair appends an unstamped line — "0=0 records=0 span=0 dt=<date>
#        archived=<stamp> repair=<ticket>" — to $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.offsets and runs the
#        archiver with SESSION_DATE=<date> ARCHIVE_JOB=repair ON_LOCK_BUSY=fail. That run reads the whole
#        retained log (a superset of every suspect range), files it under dt=<date> with
#        "recaptured_over_unproven_checkpoint" in its manifest line, and writes the stamped checkpoint the
#        next scheduled run resumes from. A hand-written offset is never resumed from, so a wrong one
#        cannot skip anything.
#     4. SUPERSEDE the old claims. Move each replaced dt=<date>/*.jsonl.gz and its _manifest.jsonl line
#        into dt=<date>/_superseded/<stamp>/ with a README naming the ticket and the replacement files.
#        The verifier reads only dt=<date>/_manifest.jsonl and the files directly under dt=<date>, so a
#        superseded capture is kept for audit but never counted twice. Then run
#        ENV=es4 oe-archive-verify.sh <date>.
#     5. Release the lock. The archive marker is recorded after the repair's capture, and re-recorded by an
#        idle run only from a stamped checkpoint that reaches the log end; the interlock checks it again
#        before any wipe.
OE_ES4_TOPICS="es.underlying.es.trades es.tape-zones.cells es.futures.cvd.bars es.futures.footprint.bars es.futures.footprint.outcomes es.futures.footprint.strike es.futures.auction"
# The es4 completeness POLICY — what oe-archive-verify.sh checks for ENV=es4 (it requires
# OE_ALL_TOPICS_<env>). The es4 job owns exactly this set, so the policy is the set itself; without
# this line the es4 floors below (including the strike floor) were declared and never checked.
OE_ALL_TOPICS_es4="$OE_ES4_TOPICS"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPLETENESS FLOORS — records per topic per trading date, below which a date is PARTIAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: "<topic>:<min_records> ..." — anything unlisted defaults to OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT.
#
# These are FLOORS, not expectations: set at roughly a tenth of an observed normal session, so a
# truncated or half-written date trips the alarm while a genuinely quiet session does not. Observed
# counts (2026-08-07, a full session) are in the comments so the next person can see what a normal
# day looks like rather than re-deriving it.
#
# ⚠️ The two feature topics are floored DELIBERATELY LOW — presence checks, not volume checks — and
# both MUST be re-derived from the first clean single-session archive. Measured 2026-08-12 on the
# first-ever capture of each:
#   delta-flow-by-strike            cleanup.policy=delete   → 873,382 records, full history intact
#   option-price-behavior-by-strike cleanup.policy=compact,delete
#                                   → 138,554 readable of 16,564,067 offsets: 0.836% SURVIVED
# OPB's history is destroyed by COMPACTION before any archiver can see it (p0: 61 readable of
# 417,095). A daily run captures mostly the active segment, which the cleaner has not touched yet,
# so its per-session yield is unknown until a full session has run — and guessing a floor now would
# either page every morning or check nothing at all. See ARCHIVE-FAIL-SILENT-FIX-20260812.md §6.
#
# es.futures.auction is one record per RTH minute. A FULL session is 390 minutes (09:30-15:59 ET),
# but an EARLY CLOSE is 210 (09:30-12:59), so the floor is 210: a floor of 390 would page on every
# half day. This floor therefore catches a dead or badly broken archive, NOT a single missing
# minute — and it cannot: the archiver counts RECORDS, while completeness for this topic means 390
# DISTINCT minute keys at their latest revision, and a restart replay re-emits keys the file
# already holds. The exact per-session check belongs to a market-calendar-aware checker that counts
# distinct keys against MarketHours' expected minutes for that date (deploy backlog, PR #994).
#
# A floor of 0 means OPTIONAL: this topic may legitimately produce nothing on a given day, and its
# absence is reported as EMPTY rather than failing the date. Use it only where silence is a real
# state of the world, never to quieten a topic you have not diagnosed — an alarm nobody trusts is
# how the original defect survived four evenings. The dealer-ledger fire topics qualify: verified on
# 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11, prod produced zero signal-fired and zero outcome-scored records on both
# days. Those are days on which no signal met threshold, not days on which archiving failed.
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT="${OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT:-1}"
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_RECORDS="\
dealer-ledger-signal-fired:0 \
dealer-ledger-outcome-scored:0 \
dealer-ledger-calibration-state:0 \
dealer-ledger-state:1000 \
dealer-ledger-profile:1000 \
close.direction.signal:1 \
options.databento.events.raw:500000 \
options.databento.display:300000 \
options.databento.gex.strike:400000 \
options.databento.gex.strike.history:150000 \
delta-flow-by-strike:1000 \
option-price-behavior-by-strike:100 \
underlying.spx.index.price:5000 \
underlying.es.price:20000 \
underlying.spx.price:1000 \
spx.basis.state:100 \
underlying.vix.index.price:50 \
es.futures.cvd.bars:500 \
es.futures.footprint.bars:500 \
es.futures.footprint.outcomes:500 \
# strike provisionally shares the source-bars floor: every well-formed bar that advances the watermark leaves
# episode records or a CHECKPOINT, so a day with 500 bars leaves at least 500 records. A coarse presence floor,
# not proof of completeness — re-derive from the first complete archive, including an early close.
es.futures.footprint.strike:500 \
es.futures.auction:210 \
underlying.vix.price:100"
